📱 Day 21: Simple Contact Book Using a Dictionary

📌 Overview

This is Day 21 of my Python learning journey.
In this project, I created a simple Contact Book using a Python dictionary.

The program allows the user to:

➕ Add a contact
👀 View a specific contact
✏️ Update a contact
🗑️ Delete a contact
📋 View all contacts
🔎 Search contacts by part of their name
🚪 Exit the program

This project goes deeper into dictionaries, which I first introduced on Day 18 with the vowel counter.



🎯 Features
1. ➕ Add Contact

The program asks for a person's name and phone number.

name = input("Enter name:").title()
number = int(input(f"Enter {name} number:"))
contact_book[name] = number

Example:

Enter name: Arun
Enter Arun number: 9876543210

Arun Contact added successfully!

The dictionary becomes:

{
    "Arun": 9876543210
}
2. 👀 View Contact

The user can search for a specific contact.

Before accessing the dictionary, the program checks whether the name exists:

if name0 in contact_book:

If found:

Arun Founded!
Arun : 9876543210

If the name doesn't exist:

Invalid name

This prevents trying to access a missing dictionary key.

3. ✏️ Update Contact

The user can update an existing phone number.

if name0 in contact_book:
    contact_book[name0] = new_number

For example:

Before:
Arun : 9876543210

After updating:
Arun : 9999999999

A dictionary makes this easy because assigning a new value to an existing key overwrites the old value.

4. 🗑️ Delete Contact

The user can delete an existing contact.

if name0 in contact_book:
    del contact_book[name0]

Example:

Before:
Arun : 9876543210
Ravi : 9876543211

Delete Arun

After:
Ravi : 9876543211

The del statement removes the entire key-value pair.

5. 📋 View All Contacts

The program loops through the dictionary:

for name in contact_book:
    print(name, ":", contact_book[name])

Example:

All Contacts:

Arun : 9876543210
Ravi : 9876543211
Priya : 9876543212

Here:

name
 ↓
"Arun"

contact_book[name]
 ↓
9876543210

So together:

print(name, ":", contact_book[name])

produces:

Arun : 9876543210


🔎 6. Search Contacts

This is the bonus feature, and I implemented it.

The program allows partial-name searching.

For example, if the contacts are:

Arun Kumar
Arun Raj
Ravi

Searching:

Enter name to search: arun

can find:

Arun Kumar : 9876543210
Arun Raj : 9999999999

The important logic is:

for name in contact_book:
    if search_text in name.lower():
        print(name, ":", contact_book[name])
Why .lower()?

Suppose the dictionary contains:

Arun

and the user enters:

ARUN

Converting both to lowercase makes the comparison easier:

"ARUN".lower()
    ↓
"arun"

"Arun".lower()
    ↓
"arun"

So the search becomes case-insensitive.



🧒 Dictionary Explained Like a 5-Year-Old

Imagine you have a little phone book 📖.

Each person's name is written on one side, and their phone number is written next to it.

┌──────────┬────────────┐
│ Name     │ Phone      │
├──────────┼────────────┤
│ Arun     │ 9876543210 │
│ Ravi     │ 9876543211 │
│ Priya    │ 9876543212 │
└──────────┴────────────┘

Python dictionary works the same way.

contact_book = {}

means:

"My phone book is empty."

Then:

contact_book["Arun"] = 9876543210

means:

"Write Arun's phone number in the phone book."

Now:

{
    "Arun": 9876543210
}

The name is the key.

The phone number is the value.

Key                 Value
 ↓                    ↓
"Arun"       →    9876543210


🧠 Dictionary Structure

The basic structure is:

dictionary[key] = value

In this project:

contact_book[name] = number

So:

Key             Value
─────────────────────────
Arun       →    9876543210
Ravi       →    9876543211
Priya      →    9876543212


🔑 Dictionary Key

A key identifies the information.

Here:

"Arun"

is the key.

We can use the key to find the phone number:

contact_book["Arun"]

Result:

9876543210

Think:

Name → Find the phone number



📞 Updating a Dictionary

One important thing about dictionaries is that the same syntax can be used for both adding and updating.

Adding
contact_book["Arun"] = 9876543210
Updating
contact_book["Arun"] = 9999999999

The old value is replaced.

Before:
Arun → 9876543210

After:
Arun → 9999999999


🔍 Checking Membership With in

The program frequently uses:

if name0 in contact_book:

This asks:

"Does this name exist in my contact book?"

For example:

if "Arun" in contact_book:

If Arun exists:

True

Otherwise:

False

This is especially useful before:

viewing
updating
deleting

a contact.



🗑️ del

The del keyword removes a key-value pair.

del contact_book["Arun"]

Before:

Arun → 9876543210
Ravi → 9876543211

After:

Ravi → 9876543211


🔄 Looping Through a Dictionary

This code:

for name in contact_book:

goes through every key in the dictionary.

For example:

contact_book = {
    "Arun": 9876543210,
    "Ravi": 9876543211
}

The loop gets:

First → Arun
Second → Ravi

Then:

contact_book[name]

gets the corresponding phone number.



🏷️ Why .title() Is Used

When entering a name:

name = input("Enter name:").title()

.title() changes the text into title case.

Example:

arun
 ↓
Arun
ravi kumar
 ↓
Ravi Kumar

This helps keep contact names consistent.



